data.content:
These are two separate C# 15 breaking changes:

---

**1. `left && right` — dynamic + user-defined short-circuit operators**

`&&` desugars into `operator false(left) ? left : operator &(left, right)`. The compiler resolves `operator false` **at compile time** (on `IFilter`), but with `right` being `dynamic`, `operator &` is resolved **at runtime**. C# 15 now rejects this as unsound — the runtime binder could pick a different `operator &` whose semantics disagree with the compile-time `operator false`.

**Fix:** avoid mixing `dynamic` with user-defined `&&`/`||`:
```csharp
// Option A: use the non-short-circuit operator (fully dynamic)
var combined = left & right;

// Option B: cast left to dynamic so everything is runtime-bound
var combined = (dynamic)left && right;

// Option C: resolve the dynamic side first, then short-circuit
IFilter r = right;
var combined = left && r;
```

---

**2. `ref readonly` lambda — CS0518 (missing predefined type)**

CS0518 means the compiler expects a predefined framework type that isn't present. C# 15 changed how `ref readonly`-returning lambdas are synthesized — it now requires a new attribute type (e.g., a `RequiresLocationAttribute` or similar metadata type) shipped in .NET 11's runtime libraries.

This typically happens when the **compiler** was upgraded to C# 15 but the **target framework** is still older. Check your project file:

```xml
<!-- Ensure BOTH are .NET 11 -->
<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
<LangVersion>15</LangVersion>
```

If you're multi-targeting or referencing an older `Microsoft.NETCore.App` ref pack, update it. If you must keep an older TFM, work around the lambda syntax:

```csharp
// Use a named method + explicit delegate instead
private delegate ref readonly int RefReadOnlyFunc();

public static void M()
{
    RefReadOnlyFunc fn = static ref readonly () => ref value;
}
```
